# What the Docker build context is allowed to contain.
#
# Two jobs. The first is speed: everything sent here is hashed and shipped to
# the daemon on every build, and most of this tree has nothing to do with
# compiling a server. The second matters more.
#
# dev/ is the private planning workspace -- the SOW, phase plans, issues,
# decisions, worklogs. It is gitignored, so it never reaches a clone, but a
# Docker build reads the working tree rather than the index: without this file
# a `COPY . .` would bake all of it into a published image layer, where anyone
# who pulls the image can read it with `docker save`. Deleting it from a later
# layer would not help; layers are additive and the earlier one is still there.
dev/

# Local secrets and TLS material. `make dev-certs` writes into certs/, and a
# config.yaml is where a real token ends up.
certs/
*.pem
*.crt
*.key
config.yaml

# Build output. The image builds its own binary from source; copying host
# binaries in would make the image's contents depend on what happened to be in
# bin/ at the time, which is the opposite of a reproducible build.
bin/
bin-*/
dist/
*.exe
*.test
*.out
*.prof
coverage.html
coverage-sdk.html

# Git metadata. Large, and the version stamp arrives through build arguments
# rather than by running git inside the builder.
.git/
.gitignore
.githooks/
.github/

# Nothing under here is compiled into the server.
docs/
examples/
test/
data/
tmp/
temp/
*.md

# The workspace file. The image builds the root module the way a consumer of
# the published module would get it, so go.work must not be present: with it,
# the build resolves pkg/client from the tree instead of from go.mod, and the
# image would be built from a different module graph than the release binaries.
go.work
go.work.sum

# Editor and OS noise.
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~

# Docker's own files. Changing a compose comment should not invalidate the
# build cache.
Dockerfile
docker-compose.yml
.dockerignore
